"Magni" <xvthreetwoonevx@yahoo.com> wrote:

> Generally, whenever I see an altar, i'll convert
> it and start sacrificing any corpses I can find to
> it.

That would be "on it", your sacrifice is _to_ your
deity.

> I'm just noticing recently, however (now that I
> survive long enough to actually kill things :-p),
> that it's pretty tough to get a corpse to an altar
> in time for it to be sacrificable.

True, you get a maximum of 50 steps, if I read
pray.c=>dosacrifice() correctly. Instead of killing
the monster far from the altar and trying to beat
the steps timeout back to the altar, the best bet is
to let the monster follow/chase you into the room
with the altar, kill it there, and thus shorten the
part of the trip where it's dead.

The down side of this is that it's a long chase for
only 1/3rd chance that the monster will drop a
corpse, and running around a lot consumes your PC's
nourishment.

You can/should balance waiting around in the room
with the altar, for monsters to arrive, with
wandering out across the level to slaughter all the
slow moving ones, like lichens, molds, shriekers,
piercers, trappers, lurkers above, clotting up the
passages, that prevents other, faster moving
monsters from finding their way to you.

The biggest problem, by far, for me at least, with
"altar camping" is the danger of starving into a
faint and then being "killed while helpless" by some
arriving monster, or plain starving to death, if you
aren't good at watching your hunger level.  Having
all the monsters blocked away from you makes your
wait longer and your chance of starving greater.

Balance "monsters sacrificed" with "monsters eaten";
eat the bigger, more nourishing ones, sacrifice the
smaller ones, unless you get satiated. Deities like
the bigger monsters as sacrifices a bit better, but
you dying to keep your deity _that_ happy isn't a
good tradeoff.

A really nice trick, if the altar isn't up against a
wall, and sometimes even if it is, is to dance
around the altar until the monster steps onto the
altar, then kill it there. Now you can sacrifice it
two turns after it's dead 1) step onto the altar 2)
#offer.

That trick is mandatory for sacrificing monsters you
cannot lift, probably owlbears, centaurs, dragons,
giants, etc., which may well be just the thing you
need to do to mollify a deity you've somehow
angered, since they expect Biggie Sized sacrifices
in that situation before they'll give you an early
reprieve from their anger.

