1) The corpses of acid blobs are sacrificable no
matter how old, until they rot away from your
inventory, so they're worth carrying back from the
farthest corner of the level, or even between levels
to a known altar.

2) An elven dagger ("runed dagger"), but not a stack
of them, can be named "Sting", giving you a dagger
with some nice special properties: it lets you cut
yourself out of spiderweb traps easily, and when
weilded, it warns you of all species of orcs
anywhere on the level, it has pluses-to-hit and
multiply-damage bonuses, but only when used against
orcs.

Hobbits tend to throw runed daggers at you a good
portion of the time you meet them.

This is one of the not-so-wonderful artifacts you'd
probably rather (but see below) receive by naming it
yourself, than by spending lots of time sacrificing
only to be disappointed in what your god drops on an
altar in response to your sacrifice.

However, Sting shouldn't get quite all the bad press
it does.

Because it is a dagger, and because almost all
classes want to exercise dagger skills, it is a
great weapon to carry around early on and use
against wimpy monsters, just to get the hits that
count as exercising your dagger skills.

There is an early period of the game where bands of
orcs are one of your bigger problems, and Sting not
only warns you against orcs anywhere on the level,
as if you had orc-specific telepthy, it has both
to-hit and damage bonuses against orcs of all kinds;
it has normal dagger characteristics against all the
rest of the monsters.

Notice that Sting is a weapon appropriate to Chaotic
PCs, it may or may not (I don't know) blast
characters of other alignments each time they first
weild it. Therefore, _don't_ be grabbing it for use
when you are very low level, or low on hit points,
it might well kill you.

3) An elven broadsword ("runed broadsword") (they
don't stack) can be named Orcrist. Orcrist is
arguably one of the worst artifacts in the game.
Though it has more base damage, as a broadsword,
than Sting has as a dagger, its enhancements against
orcs are the same to hit addition and damage
multiplier (the multipler is multiplying a bigger
original damage, though), and it _doesn't_ offer any
special protections. It's rather hilarious, too,
that orcs in chaotic roles will find it, originally
an elven weapon against orcs, to be equally useful
in killing other orcs. IMO, it should instadeath any
orc PC or orc monster who weilds it.

Elves, which travel in bands rather than by ones
usually, often have elven broadswords in their
inventory, perhaps limited to some kinds of elves.

The reason for naming Orcrist isn't to use it, it's
to bury it in a pit under a boulder, to avoid having
it used against you by a monster, cluttering up your
inventory, or being gifted to you by your deity. ;-)

Like Sting, Orcrist can probably *blast* your PC on
first weilding it each time it is weilded, if that
PC is not of chaotic alignment.

4) As others have #noted, naming Sting and Orcrist
into existence is a balancing act. The more
artifacts that have already occurred in the game,
the worse the chance to receive one as a gift from
your deity, until, I think at around 20 artifacts,
the chance drops to zero. The game has 33 total
artifacts to which those odds pertain, if I counted
correctly.  So, while getting rid of Sting and
Orcrist by #naming them is _maybe_ a good deal, the
odds of receiving each of them when it isn't wanted
isn't all that high in the first place, and if
you're going for a world record stack of artifacts,
why deliberately limit that to 18 good ones by
creating two bad ones?

[The argument's more complicated than that,
artifacts have limited usefulness and chance to
find/receive them for PCs outside their matching
alignment, but that's a starting point for a deeper
analysis.]

For me, I'd #name Sting just because I want it as an
easy way early in the game to practice dagger skills
with an artifact, not to get it out of my deity's
inventory of possible gifts, and never #name
Orcrist, just groan dramatically in the rare chance
I get it when I want something better.

Personally I tend to name the first elven dagger I
find blessed to be Sting.

